      "text": "To find the 1314th digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\), we first need to determine the repeating decimal pattern of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\).\n\nWe start by performing the division of 5 by 14:\n\n\\[\n5 \\div 14 = 0.\\overline{357142}\n\\]\n\nThis tells us that the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\) is \\(0.357142357142\\ldots\\), which means the repeating block is \"357142\". The length of the repeating block is 6 digits.\n\nNext, we need to find the position of the 1314th digit within the repeating block. We do this by determining the remainder when 1314 is divided by 6:\n\n\\[\n1314 \\div 6 = 219 \\text{ remainder } 0\n\\]\n\nA remainder of 0 indicates that the 1314th digit corresponds to the last digit of the repeating block \"357142\". The last digit of \"357142\" is 2.\n\nTherefore, the 1314th digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
