      "text": "To determine the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\), we first need to find the repeating decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\).\n\nPerforming the division \\(5 \\div 14\\), we get:\n\\[\n5 \\div 14 = 0.3571428571428571\\ldots\n\\]\nThis shows that the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\) is \\(0.\\overline{357142}\\), which means the decimal repeats every 6 digits: 357142.\n\nNext, we need to find the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit in this repeating sequence. To do this, we determine the position of the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit within the repeating cycle. We do this by finding the remainder when 1314 is divided by 6:\n\\[\n1314 \\div 6 = 219 \\text{ remainder } 0\n\\]\nA remainder of 0 means that the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit corresponds to the last digit of the repeating block 357142, which is 2.\n\nTherefore, the \\(1314^{\\text{th}}\\) digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
